\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen buoyant in thin Labor Day trade, dollar drifts.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2003\par 17:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 1 (Reuters) - The yen held close to recent highs on the dollar and the euro in thin Labor Day trading on Monday as the market tried to ascertain at what level Japanese authorities would step in to block its progress.  \par \par \uc2 After surprise news last Friday that Japan had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen in August as the market had expected, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a three-month high against the dollar with investors testing the water for how far the Bank of Japan might let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise.  \par \par \uc2 Market uncertainty over Tokyo's future policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued after non-committal statements from U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow when he visited Japan on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "We have to think whether Japan will continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  but Snow did not give a lot of news," said Hans-Guenter Redeker, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in London.  \par \par \uc2 At 1500 GMT, the yen was 0.25 percent up on the day versus the dollar at 116.55 per dollar and 0.4 percent higher on the euro at 127.90 per euro.  \par \par \uc2 It had hit session highs earlier at 116.37, only a touch below Friday's three-month high of 116.12.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar was steady versus the euro at $1.0973 after the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  drew little support from data showing euro zone manufacturing improved in August.  \par \par \uc2 European purchasing managers reported the first rise in new orders for six months, according to the August Reuters manufacturing survey (PMI), but economic recovery hopes were overshadowed by forecasts of even better performance in the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 QUESTION OF \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 In Japan, Snow praised the government's reform programme but avoided any criticism of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy by saying the U.S. welcomed economic growth anywhere. He also repeated his belief that flexible exchange rates were best for the global economy.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has spent a record nine trillion yen so far this year in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at capping the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and protecting local exporters.  \par \par \uc2 But with international pressure mounting on China and other Asian countries to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise against the dollar to help adjust the U.S. current account gap, markets are closely watching any comments Snow might make about Japan's policy as well.  \par \par \uc2 Snow was in Japan on Monday to meet officials but was going to Beijing on Tuesday to meet the head of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But meanwhile, a Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Japan and Snow had agreed they should not force China to change its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMIC RACE  \par \par \uc2 A slew of manufacturing surveys on Monday showed the euro zone economy improving but at a slower pace than the U.S., underlining the euro's recent downward trend against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Reuters Eurozone Purchasing Manufacturing index nudged closer to the 50 level that would mark a return to growth, reaching 49.1 in August from 48.0 in July.  \par \par \uc2 "The PMI numbers showed an improvement but still highlighted the point that euro zone is the lagging economy," said Tony Norfield, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at ABN Amro in London. "We do not have data from the U.S. yet but it is expected to be above 50."  \par \par \uc2 "In coming days, markets will be looking to U.S. data (manufacturing) and also any comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy regarding the yen and China."  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Institute's of Supply Management's (ISM) manufacturing index due on Tuesday is expected to show an increase to 53.8 from July's 51.8, which would be the second month running with the index above the key 50 mark.  \par \par \uc2 Other U.S. data due for release this week include non-farm payrolls on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030901dz91003l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
