\par\par \b \uc2  CHRONOLOGY-Milestones for the euro since launch. \b0\par\par\uc2 826 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2003\par 11:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (repeats adding additional information on May 12 2003) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LONDON, May 23 (Reuters) - The euro rose above its January 1999 launch\par level of $1.1747  on Friday, and also hit record highs against the yen\par just short of 138          .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Following is a chronology showing the fall and rise of Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over its short but active lifetime. \par \par \uc2  * January 1999 - Euro begins trading at $1.1747 on January 4 and rises on the same day to around $1.19, a level that remains its all-time high. It then begins a decline fuelled both by dollar strength and by a perceived reluctance by the new European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) to respond to political and market pressure for lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  * March 1999 - German Finance Minister Oskar Lafontaine resigns citing policy differences. A few days later the European Commission resigns en masse because of a financial mismanagement scandal. The euro slides three cents during the month to end at around $1.08. \par \par \uc2  * April 1999 - Euro falls below $1.08 as the ECB makes the first monetary policy move of its career, lowering interest rates by half a percentage point to 2.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  * December 1999 - Euro falls to one-for-one with the dollar. \par \par \uc2  * September 2000 - Euro falls below $0.85. Central banks in Europe, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fearing its weakness may destabilise the world economy. \par \par \uc2  * October 2000 - Effect of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  proves short-lived as euro slides to a record low of $0.8225, 30 percent below its launch value. \par \par \uc2  * November 2000 - Euro rises to $0.86 as national central banks in the euro zone \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros alongside the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  * January 2001 - Euro rises above $0.95 as concerns mount over the state of the U.S. economy and the U.S. Federal Reserve cuts interest rates by half a point to 6.0 percent, the start of an aggressive rate-cutting campaign. The euro trades in a range between $0.84 and $0.95 for the next seven months. \par \par \uc2  * September 2001 - Euro rises two percent to $0.92 on September 11 as two hijacked planes hit the twin towers of New York's World Trade Center. The euro eases towards the end of that year, ending 2001 around $0.88. \par \par \uc2  * January, 2002 - The euro leaps above $0.90 on the first trading day of the year, buoyed by a wave of enthusiasm inspired by the smooth introduction of euro notes and coins in the 12 countries of the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  * June 2002 - Euro extends gains above $0.96 as the United States shows a record current account deficit in the first quarter and U.S. telecommunications giant WorldCom and office equipment maker Xerox follow energy giant Enron in revealing accounting irregularities. \par \par \uc2  * July 2002 - Euro regains the one-to-one level against the dollar as concern over the outlook for Wall Street encourages investors to move out of U.S. assets. Euro zone finance ministers issue a formal statement saying they welcome the euro's rise. \par \par \uc2  * December 2002 - Euro ends the month near $1.05, up almost 18 percent on the year on mounting concerns that geopolitical uncertainty will make it hard for the United States to attract enough foreign investment to fund its rising trade gap. \par \par \uc2  * March 2003 - Euro rises above $1.10 on jitters ahead of an apparently imminent U.S.-led war against Iraq but slips back to $1.06 in the first week of April as a speedy U.S. victory looks likely. \par \par \uc2  * April/early May 2003 - Euro gains 10 cents against the dollar in the space of five weeks, rising above its launch levels against the yen and British pound . Dollar struggles across the board as worries over the U.S. current account and budget deficits resurface after end of the Iraq war. \par \par \uc2  * May 8 2003 - ECB President Wim Duisenberg says there is nothing excessive about the euro's current level. Euro hits record high against sterling above 71.72 pence. \par \par \uc2  * May 11 2003 - U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow notes a cheap dollar would help U.S. exports, helping lift the euro above $1.16 for first time in over four years. \par \par \uc2  * May 12 2003 - Euro hits record high against yen above 135 yen. Euro zone finance ministers and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue a joint statement saying it is in the bloc's interest to have a strong and stable euro. \par \par \uc2  * May 19 2003 - Euro comes within spitting distance of $1.1747 launch level after U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow says after a weekend G7 finance ministers meeting that recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  realignments were "fairly modest", re-enforcing speculation he was happy with a weak dollar. \par \par \uc2  * May 23 2003 - Euro rises above $1.1747 January 1999 launch level, extending gains towards $1.18, its final dash triggered by the wiping out of strongly-held sell orders around $1.1750. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030523dz5n00hwr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
