\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar slide heightens alert for yen  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 708 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2003\par 20:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 7 (Reuters) - The dollar's slide has traders on alert for possible yen-weakening market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength threatens to choke Japan's fragile economy, analysts said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The greenback's relapse to 10-month lows against the yen, around 116.10 yen on Wednesday, came after the dollar hit four-year lows against the euro on Tuesday, with investors seeking higher-yielding debt and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  No strategists reported seeing any market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday. But trading was jittery and at times rumor-driven as the dollar slipped below a key area between 116.40 and 116.30 yen, where analysts said the Bank of Japan would become more likely to act and buy dollars on behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "They could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any time with respect to the dollar. Below 118 yen we are in an amber zone. We would approach a red zone around 116 yen," said Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with the Bank of New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's session lows not far above the 116.0 mark have taken it within sight of an oft-cited "line in the sand" where many traders expect Japan would be forced to step in. \par \par \uc2  "A break of 115.0 yen would be very ominous," said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with HSBC in New York. \par \par \uc2  JAPANESE EXPORTERS HURTING \par \par \uc2  As Japan's economy struggles to attain even lackluster growth, the yen's seemingly gravity-defying ascent against the dollar has pinched Japanese exporters, whose competitiveness abroad is essential to keep the economy expanding. \par \par \uc2  Yet investors' focus on Japan's current account surplus, contrasted with the U.S. current account deficit, is boosting the yen against the dollar, overriding economic growth differentials. The yen's unbridled strength dismays Japanese businesses and politicians. \par \par \uc2  To the surprise of some U.S.-based analysts, although Japanese policy-makers are thought to be mulling a return to the market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  they orchestrated in the first quarter, the most recent protests about the yen's rise have been remarkably muted. \par \par \uc2  "The selling of the dollar against the yen we have seen in recent days (is partly because) there have been very few comments from Japanese policy-makers, and that has encouraged shorter-term investors to get involved in the trade. We do think \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks are significant given the pace of the dollar's fall against the yen," said Rebecca Patterson, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan in New York. \par \par \uc2  However, because Japan's stock markets are not currently under extreme pressure, that may stay the MOF's hand from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  for now, Patterson said. Japan's benchmark Nikkei average  finished up 0.32 percent at 8,109.77 on Wednesday, for its first five-day winning streak since late November. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said on Wednesday that current moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market were short-term and that authorities would take action if necessary. Some traders read his remarks as a measured MOF textbook-style response to yen strength, but not one that indicated imminent action. \par \par \uc2  ACTION MAY SPEAK LOUDER THAN WORDS \par \par \uc2  Yet action may soon speak louder than words if the pace of the yen's ascent accelerates and the BOJ steps in. \par \par \uc2  In the first quarter, the BOJ took a new tack and orchestrated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by stealth. It only revealed the size of its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the end of each month, long after the fact. \par \par \uc2  Statistics for April indicate the BOJ did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month. But traders are now primed for that possibility. \par \par \uc2  The pressure of dollar weakness across the board is so great that the BOJ would be unlikely to try to turn back the tide at current levels, unless thin market conditions afforded a window of opportunity to send the dollar spiking higher, said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Putnam Investments in Boston. \par \par \uc2  He said the most likely timing for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be toward the end of the U.S. trading session, before Asian markets gain their early morning momentum, and again when Tokyo volume drops before traders hit their desks in London in the European morning. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030507dz570111n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
