\par\par \b \uc2  Kuroda says in U.S. to talk about global economy. \b0\par\par\uc2 239 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 01:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, March 13 (Reuters) - Haruhiko Kuroda, senior adviser to Japan's Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, said on Thursday he was visiting the United States to discuss the state of the world economy with U.S. government officials and private sector analysts. \par \par \uc2  Asked at a briefing with reporters in Washington if the focus of his discussions on Thursday and Friday was financial market turbulence and the Iraq crisis, he said: "That's not the sole focus. The market is being affected by a number of factors and we are going to exchange views on all of those." \par \par \uc2  Kuroda, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, met with Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan as well as White House economic aides. He said he expected to talk to a number of other officials on Friday but gave no specifics. \par \par \uc2  "I came here to exchange views on the global economy with government officials as well as some private sector analysts," Kuroda told reporters. He declined to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues. \par \par \uc2  News of Kuroda's planned visit initially prompted trader speculation that he was going to the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to talk about possible coordinated yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Group of Seven nations, but Hiroshi Watanabe, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top international point person, squelched those rumors. \par \par \uc2  "Mr. Kuroda did not go to the U.S. for that kind of job," Watanabe said earlier on Thursday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030314dz3e0012z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw1