\par\par \b \uc2  Afghan leader urges countries to honour aid pledge. \b0\par\par\uc2 477 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2002\par 23:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KABUL, Oct 10 (Reuters) - President Hamid Karzai on Thursday urged countries that had promised aid to war-ravaged Afghanistan not to forget their pledges, and non-governmental organisations not to waste the money they received. \par \par \uc2  In a speech on Kabul Radio ahead of a weekend meeting at which the government will seek additional budgetary help from donors, Karzai said that of $1.8 billion in international aid pledged for the first six months of the March-to-March fiscal year, only $890 million had been received. \par \par \uc2  "I would remind those countries which have pledged us help, not to forget those pledges," he said. \par \par \uc2  "I would also like to mention that NGOs that receive donations in the name of Afghanistan should spend every single penny in the service of the people and the country, taking into account the bad condition of a country ravaged by war and the poverty of the people." \par \par \uc2  Karzai said $800 million of the funds received had gone to U.N. agencies and NGOs for reconstruction, health care, resettlement of internally displaced people and refugees returning from other countries. \par \par \uc2  He said only $90 million had been received by the government, with another $55 million due to support the introduction of a redenominated afghani \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was introduced on Monday. \par \par \uc2  He said this year's state budget would total $460 million, 80 percent of which was reliant on foreign aid. \par \par \uc2  Karzai added that $5 million had been received from the province of Herat and $100,000 from the northern city of Mazar-i-Sharif. \par \par \uc2  Foreign Minister Abdullah Abdullah said earlier this week that some countries that had pledged aid at a donors' conference in Tokyo in January had not lived up to them and others had pledged unsuitable assistance. \par \par \uc2  He said the total of $4.5 billion pledged for the next five years was insufficient and that every dollar received would have a direct impact on the security situation, both in Afghanistan and the region. \par \par \uc2  An international coalition led by the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Afghanistan to drive out the fundamentalist Taliban regime which was sheltering the al Qaeda network of Osama bin Laden, blamed for the attacks on the United States on September 11 last year. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Anwarul-Haq Ahadi told Reuters at the weekend he would like to see Afghanistan standing on its own feet economically within four to five years. \par \par \uc2  Key to this would be for international assistance to be boosted and channelled to productive projects, not just humanitarian needs, and that customs revenues from key trading provinces like Herat reached the central government. \par \par \uc2  The revenues so far received from Herat, which handles the bulk of trade from neighbouring Iran and is controlled by powerful warlord Ismail Khan, represent only a tiny fraction of the taxes pouring into the province's coffers. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021010dyaa015tp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
