\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 3-Euro climbs to $1.00 for first time since Feb 2000. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 885 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2002\par 18:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 15 (Reuters) - One euro bought more than one dollar on Monday for the first time since February 2000, rising past the parity milestone in a dramatic turnaround in fortunes for the youthful single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro, which had once sunk 30 percent below its January 1999 launch rate, has risen six percent in just a month as the greenback has been battered by a succession of U.S. corporate accounting scandals. \par \par \uc2  "The euro's push through parity gives greater credibility to the euro and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Russell Jones, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  The euro's breach of the symbolic one-to-one barrier was cheered in dealing rooms and traders saw more gains ahead. \par \par \uc2  The break came only days after European policymakers issued one of their strongest statements in support of the euro since its launch. But European Commission President Romano Prodi on Monday played down the latest move, pointing out that too high an exchange rate could also have disadvantages. \par \par \uc2  The euro, which began life at $1.1747 , first breached the $1.00 floor in December 1999, prompting jeers from those who insisted that monetary union would never work. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said recrossing parity would not bring round sceptics, for whom the exchange rate was just another stick with which to beat up the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  project. \par \par \uc2  "Parity has no economic significance. It didn't matter on the way down, it doesn't matter on the way up," said Daniel Gros, director of the Centre for European Policy Studies in Brussels. \par \par \uc2  "People who are sceptical will find a different area to criticise, they'll say growth in the euro area is too slow or the strong euro is destroying jobs." \par \par \uc2  Economists say it has been a dollar story all along and the greenback had been unassailable for most of the euro's existence, despite European policymakers shouting themselves hoarse that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had scope to appreciate. \par \par \uc2  As U.S. stock markets surged, the euro sank ever lower and in September 2000 it fell below $0.85, prompting central banks in Europe, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly for the first time in its short life to try to drive it higher. \par \par \uc2  It continued its fall to hit a record low around $0.8225 in October 2000 before finally turning the corner that November, after more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Even the debut of notes and coins at the start of this year gave the euro only a short-lived boost of "europhoria". \par \par \uc2  Now, declining U.S. stocks and questions over how long the United States can keep pulling in enough capital to offset its huge trade deficit has pushed the euro up 13 percent this year. \par \par \uc2  "EMU-friendly politicians should not get carried away with their exuberance that this vindicates their policies, it is just a small step in a more sensible level for the dollar," said Jim O'Neill, head of global economic research at Goldman Sachs. \par \par \uc2  Many traders who roared with glee when the euro crashed through the $1.00 level were equally happy cheering the move up. \par \par \uc2  "The whole market's watching this, someone's beaten me, someone's paid parity! Parity's been paid!" said a U.S. bank trader as the euro crossed the key level. \par \par \uc2  MIXED BLESSINGS \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the euro's rise would also do some of the ECB's work for it in curbing inflation. \par \par \uc2  "If this is seen as supporting growth, then you might get a virtuous circle. The rise in the euro delays interest rate rises which is seen helping recovery and so pushes the euro up further," said Michael Metcalfe, senior strategist at StateStreet. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rally could also hurt exporters competing in overseas markets. A spokeswoman for Germany's BASF , Europe's largest chemicals group, said on Monday the euro's recent strength would have both positive and negative effects. \par \par \uc2  "Exports are negatively affected by a high euro because they are more expensive in comparison to exports from the dollar area," she said. \par \par \uc2  Japan has sold yen repeatedly recently to stop its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength from derailing a fragile economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  For now, Europe is not so worried. Its economy is in better shape and it does not rely so heavily on exports. \par \par \uc2  "For European businesses, having a strong single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is good news," said Maria-Fernanda Fau, spokeswoman for UNICE, which speaks for more than 16 million firms trading in Europe. \par \par \uc2  "There are companies who sell things outside and they will see a negative effect but a lot of trade is done between euro zone countries." \par \par \uc2  The euro's gains were also cheered by campaigners wanting Britain to join the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as sterling also loses ground to Europe's resurgent common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We are now much nearer to an exchange rate that economists believe would be suitable for Britain to join," said Lord Haskins, a member of the pro-euro lobby Britain in Europe. \par \par \uc2  But perhaps happiest were Europeans planning U.S. holidays. \par \par \uc2  "We had been hoping for this for a long time," said Milena Rosa, 31, in Rome. "It will cost us less to go to America." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020715dy7f00nro\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
