\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Japan needs help to win battle against yen's rise. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Nishikawa \par \uc2 983 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2002\par 12:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 27 (Reuters) - Japan looks to be losing its battle to keep the yen from rising to levels where authorities fear it will douse an export-led recovery, and the longer it goes it alone the less the chance of success, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  It's not a question of fire power. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan has in the order of 20 trillion yen ($167 billion) at its disposal to dip into the market to buy dollars whenever it is asked to by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The real problem, apart from a relentless trend for a weaker dollar, is a certain lack of credibility - something that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with other countries would bring to the process. \par \par \uc2  "It is hard to stop the dollar's slide by solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when there is momentum in the market for a weaker dollar," said Seiji Shiraishi, chief market economist at Daiwa Securities SMBC. \par \par \uc2  Over the past few weeks, Japan has spent an estimated $25 billion buying dollars in a market that just wants to sell them. \par \par \uc2  The stampede out of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has seen the yen rise almost 12 percent against the dollar since April 1, with no let-up in sight as foreigners exit U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan, which acts as the Finance Ministry's agent, sold yen - probably the equivalent of $4 billion or so - in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Wednesday as the dollar skidded towards a psychologically key level of 120 yen. \par \par \uc2  The impact was short-lived. Just hours later, in overseas trading, the dollar slid to a nine-month low of 118.90 yen. \par \par \uc2  It was at 119.75 in late Tokyo trading on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that unless other nations made it clear they shared Tokyo's concerns about a weak dollar, or joined in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Japan alone would not be able to reverse the trend. \par \par \uc2  MESSAGE TO THE MARKET \par \par \uc2  "Even if other central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of Japan in overseas trade, that may not bring a strong message to the market," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  History shows that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been much more effective, according to a study by Takatoshi Ito, an economist at Hitotsubashi University in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Using data from April 1991 to March 2001, Ito concluded that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Tokyo and Washington had been 20-50 times more effective than solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japan. \par \par \uc2  In a round of Japanese-led \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in late September - when the dollar was also trading around 120 yen - the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) and New York Federal Reserve operated on behalf of the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  But Tokyo has been acting alone in the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May and June. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar buying orders were placed with overnight desks of Japan's major banks, even when the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was during European and New York trading time. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials say the decision on whether or not to use other central banks on behalf of the BOJ has nothing to do with the market's suspicion that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Europe do not support Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts. \par \par \uc2  With computer dealing systems, they say, sometimes it is faster and easier to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  without asking other central banks to get involved. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's slide has been attributed largely to a sell-off on Wall Street and eroding trust in U.S. business practices after a series of scandals. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a massive U.S. current account deficit and Japan's rising current account surplus suggested the dollar will stay on a weakening trend, giving the yen room to rise further. \par \par \uc2  However, economists such as Nikko Salomon Smith Barney's Tomoko Fujii see 115 yen as Japan's make or break line that it would do everything to defend. \par \par \uc2  The United States, she says, would probably not get involved in any joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the sell-off of the dollar turned into a panic that threatened the financial system. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo fears that an excessive rise in the yen could hurt its tenuous export-led recovery by eroding the yen value of Japanese firms' overseas earnings and inflame deflationary pressures. \par \par \uc2  But other nations appear to be cool to Tokyo's view. \par \par \uc2  "The United States would not panic with the current \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels," said Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi's Fukaya. \par \par \uc2  "As the U.S. business sector thinks a weaker dollar could help them, the United States does not have a reason to push up the dollar now," he said. \par \par \uc2  STRONG DOLLAR POLICY INTACT, MAYBE \par \par \uc2  A U.S. Treasury spokesman said on Wednesday that the U.S. strong-dollar policy remained intact, but many dealers remain suspicious that Washington is happy with a weaker dollar. \par \par \uc2  European officials have also said they see no immediate threat from the recent ascent of the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has risen to near parity against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  German Finance Minister Hans Eichel on Wednesday welcomed the recent rise in the euro, repeating that a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should keep European interest rates low. \par \par \uc2  European Monetary Affairs Commissioner Pedro Solbes chipped in, saying the euro's rise would not have a negative impact on European economies in the medium term. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, on Thursday tried to brush aside the view that Japan is in a lonely battle. \par \par \uc2  "G7 nations will make efforts for a stable recovery in the world economy. That remains unchanged," Kuroda told reporters. \par \par \uc2  His boss, Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, was less convincing. The minister said on Wednesday he had not asked Washington to help Tokyo in its efforts to stem the yen's rapid rise, though he noted that recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were not Japan's problem alone. \par \par \uc2  "Other nations do not show what they really think. It is important that we collect information," he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020627dy6r00l57\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
