\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ goes solo with yen-selling  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  in US. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 923 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2002\par 07:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 31 (Reuters) - Japan's monetary authorities staged a sneak yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during U.S. hours Friday without help from their counterparts at the Federal Reserve, a move analysts said was not surprising. \par \par \uc2  "It's possible Japan did not want to get the Fed involved because they were afraid a request might be received coolly," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEAglobal, while noting the Fed certainly would have cooperated if asked. \par \par \uc2  "It's also possible that Japan wanted to do it on their own to avoid politicizing the issue in the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Fed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have had the media asking questions and opening up a whole new level of jawboning (on dollar policy) that is not necessary," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed the Bank of Japan bought dollars for yen during U.S. hours Friday following waves of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia and Europe overnight. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were no signs the BOJ acted through banks in the United States and suggested it placed orders with overnight desks of dealing banks in Tokyo. The MOF did not say how the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was done. \par \par \uc2  THERE'S A STRONG DOLLAR AND THEN THERE'S A STRONG DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Fed aiding of the BOJ, even it were simply at Japan's request, might have been inferred by the market as reflecting a desire on the part of the government for a stronger dollar. \par \par \uc2  U.S. officials repeatedly have maintained that a strong dollar is in U.S. interests, while taking pains to avoid suggesting it wanted the dollar to be stronger than it is now. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, in an interview published in the Wall Street Journal's online edition Friday, White House economic adviser Lawrence Lindsey shrugged of the dollar's recent sharp declines against the euro. He noted the dollar on a trade-weighted basis was just three percent off its top hit earlier this year, a peak which had put it 33 percent above levels in 1995. \par \par \uc2  "You don't get the sense that anybody sees a major issue in this administration with the dollar," said one market watcher. \par \par \uc2  "That only happens when you have major moves. I wouldn't exclude it if we saw a very weak dollar. It's hard to argue that we have a very weak dollar." \par \par \uc2  Japan is worried the yen's roughly eight percent appreciation versus the dollar this year will hurt its exports, which have been a key driver in the economy's recovery. \par \par \uc2  But traders say this is Japan's problem and not a major worry for either the United States or Europe. \par \par \uc2  There has been no sign the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has aided Japan when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during European hours. \par \par \uc2  "It seems that Japan is quite happy to go it alone," said IDEAglobal's Callow. "If they really want to get the others on board, then they can lobby the G7 (Group of Seven leading industrial nations) when it meets in June. Historically, that is how they have done it." \par \par \uc2  JUST A TECHNICALITY \par \par \uc2  Technically speaking, BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during U.S. hours should be done through the Fed. \par \par \uc2  But a Bank of Japan source in New York said it was quite possible, and even normal, for the BOJ to call commercial banks in Tokyo during New York trading hours and leave \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  orders with them. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ would then immediately contact the Fed to advise it of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ did just that last September, when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during U.S. hours through banks in Tokyo as part of its effort to weaken the yen after it soared to around 116 per dollar following the Sept. 11 attacks on the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  started Friday during Asian hours when the dollar was below 123 yen, a six-month low, and continued during European hours. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose as high as 124.59 yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimated the BOJ spent some $5 billion to $7 billion in those \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was the third day of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the bank following actions Wednesday and Thursday of last week, in which bankers figured it bought $8.6 billion in dollars. \par \par \uc2  Bank of America Chief \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Eric Nickerson said the BOJ picked the right moment during U.S. hours, coming in just moments after the release of surprisingly good U.S. factory orders and regional manufacturing data spurred buying of dollars across the board. \par \par \uc2  "They were trying to take advantage of a thin market so they hopefully wouldn't have to spend so much," he said. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume was especially light Friday with a four-day holiday weekend getting under way in Britain and the start of the World Cup thinning the ranks of trading desks. \par \par \uc2  Nickerson said Japan's task remained a tough one. With investors reweighting their portfolios more toward neutral after years of being underweight Japan, demand for yen would only increase. \par \par \uc2  The job could get even harder next week, if Japan's first-quarter gross dometic product numbers show an economy well on the mend. Economists on average foresee annualized growth of 6.2 percent, even better than the 5.6 percent posted by the United States. \par \par \uc2  But Callow said Japan has a strong weapon in that it is selling its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and thus is building, rather than depleting, foreign reserves. \par \par \uc2  "It's almost a limitless strategy. And they've shown that they have the stomach for a long fight," he added. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020601dy61005py\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
