\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar steady vs yen, markets wary of BOJ action. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2001\par 10:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 24 (Reuters) - The dollar held steady within half a percent of recent seven-month lows against the yen on Monday as wariness of further Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap yen strength offset continuing risk aversion. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan sold yen for dollars on three days last week and dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could take advantage of illiquid trading conditions because of a holiday in Japan to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Swiss franc retreated from Friday's 20-month peaks against the dollar and record highs versus the euro. However, economists saw its losses as short-lived with the focus remaining on the fallout from the September 11 attacks on the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "At the moment, there is a chance of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japanese authorities are unlikely to be happy with dollar/yen at current levels," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief European economic advisor at Bank of America in London. \par \par \uc2  "The Swiss franc is retreating but the possibility of U.S. air strikes this week could renew safe-haven flows into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  At 0722 GMT, the dollar stood around 116.55 yen, little changed from late New York levels and compared with Thursday's seven-month troughs around 115.80. \par \par \uc2  LOOKING OUT FOR BOJ \par \par \uc2  The yen has added some five percent against the dollar in the wake of the attacks on the United States, fuelling concerns in Tokyo that a firm yen will harm a frail domestic economy. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ on Friday took the unusual step of buying dollars during New York trading hours, albeit only through Japanese banks in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Since the BOJ had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asian hours as well on Friday, traders suspected the total amount purchased could be upwards of $10 billion - making it more than the combined amount of dollars bought during last Monday and Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The danger of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still there. The BOJ made it clear they will come in even when Tokyo is closed," said one European bank dealer. "The dollar is struggling to go above 117 and we are probably going to test the downside today." \par \par \uc2  Yen bulls are also keeping a wary eye on Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's meeting with U.S. President George W. Bush on Tuesday, in case \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are mentioned. Dealers, however, believed talk on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could take a back seat given the events of recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  Part of the yen's recent rally is attributed to unwinding of yen carry trades, where investors borrowed at low interest rates in yen to lend at higher rates in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly the euro. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood around 106.45 yen, within sight of two-week lows just above 106 yeb set on Friday. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR REBOUNDS VS SWISSIE \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood near the day's peaks around 1.5875 Swiss francs, recovering more than one percent from Friday's 20-month lows as traders locked in profits. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also held back after the Swiss National Bank reduced overnight lending rates in a bid to cap its strength. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc has been a major beneficiary of safe-haven flows triggered by heightened global uncertainty in the aftermath of the assults on the United States. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc stood around 1.4500 per euro having rallied almost five percent since the air plane attacks. \par \par \uc2  Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meanwhile, held near the day's lows around $0.9120 after falling over a cent on Friday from $0.9275. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said concerns over the fallout from the attacks on the euro zone economy were starting to take a toll on the euro. \par \par \uc2  Belgian Finance Minister Didier Reynders, who currently chairs meetings the Eurogroup of euro zone finance ministers, said on Monday the economic impact of the attacks was likely to bring euro zone growth below two percent this year. \par \par \uc2  Just how cautious consumers have become since the attacks on the United States should be illustrated by the U.S. Conference Board confidence survey due on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010924dx9o00h4y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
