\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar defensive ahead of U.S. stocks open. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 745 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 14:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to fresh six-month lows against the euro and eight-month lows on the Swiss franc on Monday as traders braced for the reopening of U.S. stock markets following last week's attacks on the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Even a bout of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japan failed to give the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  any lasting support and the greenback was soon within sight of earlier seven-month lows. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street returns later today from its longest shutdown since the Great Depression and worries of a sell-off were heightened by hefty falls in European and Japanese stocks. Japan's benchmark Nikkei stock index closed at a fresh 17-year low. \par \par \uc2  "All eyes are on the re-opening of the U.S. stock market," said Nick Stamenkovic, senior strategist at Nomura International in London. \par \par \uc2  "The overall backdrop is continued uncertainty, so we're seeing volatility in equity markets and this is pressuring the dollar." \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials said their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stemmed from worries that yen strength may harm Japan's already frail economy. The yen has added almost five percent against the dollar in the past week. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  served as a warning that excessive dollar bearishness could be punished, although they added that it had been conducted for domestic reasons. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell more than one percent to six-month troughs around $0.9330 before trimming losses to around $0.9270 by 1150 GMT. \par \par \uc2  It hit eight-month lows around 1.5960 Swiss francs and hovered around 117 yen, within sight of earlier seven-month lows around 116.70 yen. \par \par \uc2  However, evidence that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market nervousness extended beyond the dollar came with the euro's descent to all time lows against the Swiss franc around 1.4870 francs. \par \par \uc2  "There is real, nervous anticipation of the opening in U.S. equity markets. The reaction of the Swiss franc has been evidence of a growing risk aversion in world markets," said Kamal Sharma, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank in London. \par \par \uc2  Volumes were said to be thin, around 50 percent down on a normal trading day, exposing the market to heightened volatility. \par \par \uc2  U.S. STOCKS TAKE CENTRE STAGE \par \par \uc2  There was some uncertainty over how Wall Street will respond to last week's events in the United States although hotel, insurance and airline stocks were widely thought to be among the most vulnerable in any sell-off. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the Federal Reserve would be monitoring the situation closely and that immediate interest rate cuts were a possibility in the event of hefty losses. \par \par \uc2  There was also talk that major central banks could coordinate monetary easing to win back confidence in the world economy. \par \par \uc2  The Fed was widely expected to cut interest rates by as much as half a percentage point before long in any case. The BOJ begins a two-day policy meeting on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar showed little immediate reaction to comments by U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill that the economy would recover despite inevitable disruptions caused by last week's terror attacks. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg reiterated the ECB was ready to support smooth functioning of markets. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENES\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Ministry, through the Bank of Japan, bought dollars for yen after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell below 117 yen, shaken by uncertainty over the U.S. economy in the aftermath of last week's destructive attacks on New York and Washington. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been undertaken because the yen's recent sharp gains could have "undesirable implications for the recovery of Japan's economy". \par \par \uc2  While the total size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not immediately clear, dealers said the BoJ had been conducting a wave of dollar buying during the second half of Japanese trade. This continued into the start of the European session, according to an international monetary source. \par \par \uc2  "The BoJ's been alone overnight on dollar/yen and it's been the only buyer holding it up. But the authorities do not want a run on the dollar," said one trader. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have repeatedly warned they could act at any time to cap the yen's strength, so their action on Monday did not come as a huge surprise to the market. \par \par \uc2  The Fed, BoJ and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  together in September 2000 in a joint bid to rescue a frail euro. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010917dx9h00oe2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
