\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Greenspan's remarks dent dollar, euro benefits. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2001\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (freshens, updates prices) \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 26 (Reuters) - The dollar lost some of this week's gains against the euro on Friday as the market digested U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's sombre assessment of the slowing U.S. economy the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Greenspan's assessment that U.S. growth may have slowed close to zero forced the dollar to reverse its course of a day ago and extend those losses on Friday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  However, the dollar, which had at one point shed more than two percent of its value from Thursday's one-month highs against the euro, clawed back ground toward New York's closing levels amid profit-taking ahead of U.S. durable goods data at 1330 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was underpinned by a batch of upbeat comments from European officials, including Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke and directorate member Edgar Meister, who said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not need a cut in interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Their remarks contrasted sharply with Greenspan's comments, which were seen signalling a U.S. rate cut next week and endorsing President George W. Bush's tax cut plans. \par \par \uc2  "The euro had been struggling all week against the dollar but people are now focusing on Greenspan's remarks on zero growth and that is why the euro is doing better," said Audrey Childe-Freeman, economist at CIBC World Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment, because the euro is going higher, the prospect of no rate change in the euro zone seemes to be good news for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The euro was hovering around $0.9250/60 by 1245 GMT, more than half a cent below earlier highs around $0.9317 , according to Reuters data. It was still up more than one cent above Thursday's one-month lows around $0.9110. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hovering near the day's lows around 107.70 yen, down more than half a yen on the day, but still up one yen from three-week lows set on Thursday around 106.65 . \par \par \uc2  "The euro seems to have little momentum of its own and it had picked up a bit on the market's reassessment of the dollar," said Elizabeth Legge, economist at Bankgesellschaft Berlin in London. \par \par \uc2  "After nine years of positive growth and a lot of euphoria, I am pessimistic about U.S. growth this year and I think Greenspan is right to be cautious and for now the dollar will remain under pressure." \par \par \uc2  The Bundesbank's Welteke said there was no pressure on the ECB to act even if the Fed cut rates by 50 basis points and that he still thought Germany could achieve 2001 growth of 2.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  These followed comments from Meister who also said he remained optimistic about the prospects for the German economy, and there were few signs that would suggest an abrupt end to the economic upswing, despite some dampening factors. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the ECB's chief economist Otmar Issing said the euro would get a long-term boost from euro-denominated bond redemptions and by returns from the recent wave of European corporate investment in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone December M3 money suppy growth slowed to 4.9 pct, but the growth was slightly higher than expected and well above the ECB's 4.5 pct reference rate. \par \par \uc2  After Greenspan's comments about zero growth, analysts said a half percentage rate cut at next week's Federal Open Market Committee was practically a done deal. \par \par \uc2  But some analysts said the prospect of an aggressive credit easing and a tax cut would help avoid a hard landing for the U.S. economy, helping to steady the dollar on the longer-term horizon. \par \par \uc2  "Lower interest rates and tax cuts help diminish the hard-landing risks for the U.S. and that will contribute to a steadier dollar," said Neil MacKinnon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, an earlier newspaper report that U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill said he was opposed to government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets reinforced the market's impression he will stick to Washington's long-standing strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market did not move on the report but some dealers said the comment was seen as dollar-supportive at the margin, since it underlines the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' reluctance to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to either lift the euro or limit the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  The yen was steady around 116.50 against the dollar, despite the prevailing bearishness on the Japanese economy, with dealers citing fund repatriation by Japanese companies ahead of their fiscal year-end in March as a source of support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx1q076c0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
