\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro firms as market digests Greenspan comments. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2001\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 26 (Reuters) - The euro edged up against the dollar on Friday as the market digested U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's sombre assessment of the slowing U.S. economy the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan's assessment that U.S. growth may have slowed close to zero illustrated the pressure the dollar faced, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The euro hit the day's highs after a Bundesbank official said he was optimistic on the German economy and the euro zone did not need a cut in interest rates, contrasting Greenspan's remarks which were seen signalling a U.S. rate cut next week and endorsing President George W. Bush's tax cut plans. \par \par \uc2  "To the extent that Fed easing is going to be a lot more than the ECB (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), this should be euro supportive," said Jeremy Hawkins, senior economic advisor at Bank of America in London. \par \par \uc2  "If Greenspan is thinking about rate cuts and tax cuts, this might suggest the economy is slowing down more than expected." \par \par \uc2  Some analysts saw the prospect of an aggressive credit easing and a tax cut soothing concerns about a hard-landing for the U.S. economy, helping to steady the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Lower interest rates and tax cuts help diminish the hard-landing risks for the U.S. and that will contribute to a steadier dollar," said Neil MacKinnon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  "In the meantime, the dollar is just going to be in its recent trading range. The main theme in the past week has been changes in market positions which resulted in a slippage in the euro and speculative traders unwinding short yen positions. A lot of that is probably complete." The euro was trading at around $0.9280 at 0845 GMT, up more than a quarter cent from late Thursday levels and more than a cent off Thursday's one-month lows. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen orbited the 116.80 yen area, virtually unchanged on the day and slightly above Monday's two-week troughs near 116.10. \par \par \uc2  FED SEEN CUTTING RATES NEXT WEEK \par \par \uc2  After Greenspan's comments about zero growth, analysts said a half percentage rate cut at next week's Federal Open Market Committee was practically a done deal. \par \par \uc2  "The question is how much of that has already been priced into the market. Looking at the futures market it is largely priced in," said Matthew Clements, economist at Prebon Marshall Yamane in London. \par \par \uc2  "People had pretty much expected it (Greenspan's stance), but it also opens the door to more rate cuts afterwards, which people weren't necessarily expecting." \par \par \uc2  Earlier, a newspaper report that U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill said he was opposed to government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets reinforced the market's impression that he will stick to Washington's longstanding strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with the Wall Street Journal, O'Neill said he did not much like \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although he added, "Are there exceptions to every rule? Probably." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates did not move on the report, but some dealers said the comment was seen as dollar-supportive at the margin since it underlines the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' reluctance to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to either lift the euro or limit the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  The yen was holding its ground against the dollar, despite the prevailing bearishness on the Japanese economy, with dealers citing fund repatriation by Japanese companies ahead of their fiscal yearend in March as a source of support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I feel that such repatriation demand from Japanese investors ahead of book-closing at the end of March has increased recently," said Yutaka Kawazoe, manager of Industrial Bank of Japan's international treasury department. \par \par \uc2  Still, data out this morning illustrated the depth of Japan's troubles. \par \par \uc2  Nationwide retail sales fell 0.9 percent in December, the 45th consecutive month of year-on-year falls, while overall consumer prices fell 0.7 percent for the whole of 2000, the largest drop on record. \par \par \uc2  Worries over the Japanese economy were reflected in the recent surge in JGB prices, with the key yield falling to its lowest level since May 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx1q076an\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
