\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro regains foothold as Greenspan deflates dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 741 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2001\par 13:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 26 (Reuters) - The euro rose against the dollar on Friday, recouping some of this week's losses, as the market digested U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's sombre assessment of the slowing U.S. economy the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Greenspan's assessment that U.S. growth may have slowed close to zero forced the dollar to reverse its course of a day ago and extend those losses on Friday. It shed more than two percent of its value from one-month highs it set against the euro on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise was aided by a batch of upbeat comments from European officials, including Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke and directorate member Edgar Meister, who said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not need a cut in interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Their remarks contrasted sharply with Greenspan's comments, which were seen signalling a U.S. rate cut next week and endorsing President George W. Bush's tax cut plans. \par \par \uc2  "The euro had been struggling all week against the dollar but people are now focusing on Greenspan's remarks on zero growth and that is why the euro is doing better," said Audrey Childe-Freeman, economist at CIBC World Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment, because the euro is going higher, the prospect of no rate change in the euro zone seemes to be good news for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The euro rose as far as $0.9317 by 1043 GMT, according to Reuters data. It was up more than half a percent from New York's closing levels on Thursday and more than two cents above Thursday's one-month lows around $0.9110. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains against the dollar lifted it to the day's highs above 108.55 yen, up nearly two yen from three-week lows set on Thursday around 106.65. \par \par \uc2  WELTEKE SEES GROWTH AT 2.75 PERCENT \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank's Welteke said there was no pressure on the ECB to act even if the Fed cut rates by 50 basis points and that he still thought Germany could achieve 2001 growth of 2.75 percent. These followed comments from Meister who also said he remained optimistic about the prospects for the German economy, and there were few signs that would suggest an abrupt end of the economic upswing, despite some dampening factors. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the ECB's chief economist Otmar Issing said the euro would get a long-term boost from euro-denominated bond redemptions and by returns from the recent wave of European corporate investment in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone December M3 money suppy growth slowed to 4.9 percent, but the growth was slightly higher than expected and well above the ECB's 4.5 percent reference rate. \par \par \uc2  "To the extent that Fed easing is going to be a lot more than the ECB, this should be euro supportive," said Jeremy Hawkins, senior economic advisor at Bank of America in London. \par \par \uc2  "If Greenspan is thinking about rate cuts and tax cuts, this might suggest the economy is slowing down more than expected." \par \par \uc2  After Greenspan's comments about zero growth, analysts said a half percentage rate cut at next week's Federal Open Market Committee was practically a done deal. \par \par \uc2  But some analysts said the prospect of an aggressive credit easing and a tax cut would help avoid a hard landing for the U.S. economy, helping to steady the dollar on the longer-term horizon. \par \par \uc2  "Lower interest rates and tax cuts help diminish the hard-landing risks for the U.S. and that will contribute to a steadier dollar," said Neil MacKinnon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, an earlier newspaper report that U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill said he was opposed to government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets reinforced the market's impression he will stick to Washington's longstanding strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market did not move on the report, but some dealers said the comment was seen as dollar-supportive at the margin, since it underlines the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' reluctance to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to either lift the euro or limit the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  The yen was steady against the dollar, despite the prevailing bearishness on the Japanese economy, with dealers citing fund repatriation by Japanese companies ahead of their fiscal yearend in March as a source of support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx1q074qk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
