\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. election saga weighing on euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Javier David \par \uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2000\par 21:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Nov 28 (Reuters) - The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (ECB) campaign to staunch its single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's persistent declines is hamstrung each day the United States is without a president-elect, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - particularly with the participation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other Group of Seven industrialized nations - is a key factor preventing the market from aggressively selling the ailing euro. \par \par \uc2  But the protracted presidential election - now three weeks old - has shoved concerns about joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  onto the back burner, and every day the drama drags on the fear of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lessens some, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "I would be surprised if the ECB had even approached the U.S. around the time of the elections," said Andrew Delano, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at IDEAGLOBAL.com, a research firm in New York, saying Washington would be highly reluctant to be seen weakening the dollar at a time of election turmoil. \par \par \uc2  "We have an environment in which there's a changing of the guard, so would Europe really want to risk being denied?" Delano said. \par \par \uc2  The ECB and its Group of Seven partners - The United States, Britain, France, Germany, Japan, Italy and Canada - swooped into the market in September to buy euros, lifting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by several cents to above 90 cents per euro. \par \par \uc2  Since then, the ECB has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone on three separate occasions early this month. But the results have been more limited, with the euro's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -led gains evaporating as soon as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left the market. \par \par \uc2  Losing ground after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not a tragedy as long as the market remains nervous about the risk of fresh action, said Eric Nickerson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Banc of America Securities in New York. \par \par \uc2  "A major element of what (the ECB) is doing is to buy time so at some stage they can actually present again the risk of a G7 threat," Nickerson said. "But the election has gone on longer than anyone could have anticipated, and that has probably been a detriment to their strategy." \par \par \uc2  JITTERS PERSIST \par \par \uc2  The ECB can be grateful, however, that even acting alone still has some resonance in the market. \par \par \uc2  Last week the euro halted two weeks of steady declines at around 84 cents per euro, and this week actually has pushed some two cents higher to a peak this session near 85.80 cents. \par \par \uc2  Traders cited lingering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears and, ironically, the market's discomfort with the protracted election dispute pitting Republican George W. Bush against Democrat Al Gore. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank President Hans Welteke reminded the market that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains an option, saying the ECB can enter the market again when it sees fit. \par \par \uc2  Analysts warned, however, that the ECB will have to be careful not to overplay its hand when it acts alone. \par \par \uc2  "Repeated unsuccessful actions just erode credibility," Nickerson said. "On one hand, they can't do it too often, but on the other, they cannot let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  get too weak either." \par \par \uc2  Of course, nothing is preventing the current U.S. administration from helping the ECB prop up the euro. \par \par \uc2  "(Lawrence) Summers is still Treasury Secretary, (Bill) Clinton is still president," said Steven Cerier, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Standard & Poor's MMS. "There (may be) confusion over who's the next president, there's not any confusion over present economic policy of the present administration." \par \par \uc2  But such a scenario remains unlikely, not only because of the election uncertainty but also because Washington was a reluctant participant in the first place, he said. \par \par \uc2  Looking forward, Bush is seen more euro-bearish if he wins the keys to the White House because he is seen as less likely to back further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  than Gore. \par \par \uc2  But neither candidate is expected to change the current "strong dollar" policy which is seen keeping inflation and interest rates in check. \par \par \uc2  "The policy of the U.S. is a strong dollar - that has not changed and it will not change, no matter who is president," said Cerier. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwbs00ivf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
