\par\par \b \uc2  IMM  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  end mixed, yen drops sharply. \b0\par\par\uc2 421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2000\par 22:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, Nov 20 (Reuters) - IMM \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures finished mixed, with the yen ending sharply lower even though Japanese Prime Minister Yoshiro Mori survived a no-confidence vote. \par \par \uc2  Yen futures rallied from session lows immediately after the no-confidence vote, with the nearby December contract rising from its new contract low of $0.009106, which was more than $0.000100 below its prior contract low. \par \par \uc2  "It came off the low after Mori survived, but the yen is taking it on the chin," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures trader on the floor of the Chicago Mercantile Exchange. \par \par \uc2  Participants still viewed the political situation in Japan as negative for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the trader said, because Mori "will be in such a weakened condition that he won't be able to get any of his structural reform through." \par \par \uc2  Despite the decline, longer-term charts on the yen show it still remains within a broad trading range in place since mid-September, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Overall volume in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures was light as traders awaited a ruling by the Florida Supreme Court on whether recounted ballots can be used in the Florida election certification of votes. \par \par \uc2  Nearly two weeks have passed since the U.S. presidential election without a declared winner, and the outcome hinges on the Florida vote. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone is waiting for the ruling from the Florida Supreme Court," the floor trader said. \par \par \uc2  Other analysts noted that strength in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures was limited despite the steep declines in U.S. equities markets, which partly reflected a wariness of participants to take on positions while the outcome of the election is in doubt. \par \par \uc2  The euro ended slightly higher as traders remained concerned about the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The euro is a wait-and-see game," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at a U.S. brokerage house, noting that concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would include the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  is doubtful right now with the outcome of the election uncertain. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian and Australian dollar contracts ended lower, falling to near contract lows, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained well within down-channels. Analysts said the negative chart outlook for both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keeping buyers sidelined. \par \par \uc2  December yen settled off $0.000089 at $0.009142, euros rose 0.00017 to $0.85040, Swiss francs rose $0.0012 to $0.5581, sterling was unchanged at $1.4238, Australian dollars dropped $0.0049 to $0.5145, Mexican pesos gained $0.000525 to $0.105300 and Canadian dollars fell $0.0004 to $0.6423. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwbk01b9y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
