\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro steady, propped by  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  wariness. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2000\par 09:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 14 (Reuters) - The euro was steady on Tuesday, trapped in a tight range against the dollar amid wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and uncertainty surrounding last week's U.S. election. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the euro three times in the past two weeks, and traders remain vigilent for further action. \par \par \uc2  Traders were also mindful of the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of a meeting of officials from the Group of Seven industrial nations in Paris later today and after comments from Bank of England Governor Eddie George on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Speaking after a meeting of G10 central bankers in Mexico, George said he could not exclude the chance of further concerted Group of Seven \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though he added the caveat: "I don't know that that is imminent." \par \par \uc2  "The ECB has shown it is willing to come in at any time to stem the euro's decline," said Tamiko Bayliss, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank in London. \par \par \uc2  "There is still a huge appetite for dollars despite political confusion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  means traders are reluctant to drive the euro lower." \par \par \uc2  Speculation was rife that the G7 could discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though their main agenda was aid for the Balkans. Dealers were all the more wary as joint G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the euro's support on September 22 came a day before a G7 meeting. \par \par \uc2  The euro was hovering around $0.86 at 0835 GMT, little changed from late New York levels around $0.8592 but down from Monday's highs around $0.8665. \par \par \uc2  U.S. POLITICAL SAGA DRAGS ON \par \par \uc2  Barring \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets would be driven by the twists and turns of the unfolding U.S. presidential drama. \par \par \uc2  In the United States, the imposition of a Tuesday deadline for vote recounting in Florida appeared to favour Republican George W. Bush. \par \par \uc2  But this deadline was challenged in the courts where a decision is due around 1530 GMT on Tuesday. Analysts assume that if a full manual count is allowed it would tend to favour Al Gore. \par \par \uc2  Bush is generally considered as less likely to support G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  than Gore, which is why the euro tends to slip when events in the election swing his way. \par \par \uc2  YEN RESILIENT DESPITE POLITICAL CONFUSION \par \par \uc2  The political situation in Japan, meanwhile, was fast catching up with the United States in terms of sheer confusion, although the yen proved resilient, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Embattled Prime Minister Yoshiro Mori defied calls for him to step aside but his main challenger, Koichi Kato, showed no signs of backing down and the opposition pressed ahead with plans for a no-confidence vote. \par \par \uc2  The yen edged ahead to 92.66 per euro from Monday's highs around 93.40 while holding steady on the dollar around 107.70. \par \par \uc2  The situation was bad enough for Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa to warn that a political vacuum might harm Japan's economy at a critical stage in its recovery. \par \par \uc2  But dealers saw longer term benefits if the deeply unpopular Mori was replaced by a more reformist leader. \par \par \uc2  "The political issue could drag the yen lower short term, but we have to see the next step. The next step might be a new prime minister who is better than the present one, which would be yen positive," said Karl Broeker, head of Treasury at Landesbank Baden-Wuerttenburg. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwbe024se\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
