\par\par \b \uc2  Pound rangebound as U.S. election saga dominates. \b0\par\par\uc2 337 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2000\par 09:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The pound was trapped in recent ranges on Monday as traders focused on the U.S. presidential election saga and the prospect of further European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Democrats have requested a manual recount of votes in four Florida counties, though lawyers from Republican George W. Bush's camp are trying to block the count in the courts. The recount is expected to favour Vice President Al Gore. \par \par \uc2  "The big issues in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are outside the UK," said Kit Juckes, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Royal Bank of Scotland Financial Markets. "Even a raft of important British data this week is unlikely to push sterling out of its recent trading range. \par \par \uc2  The pound was trading at 60.35 pence per euro at 0820 GMT, little changed from Friday's European close and within half a penny of seven-week lows hit on Friday around 60.80 pence. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, sterling was quoted around $1.4320 , and traders said they expected it to hold its recent $1.4200-$.1.4400 range. \par \par \uc2  Sterling has weakened across the board in recent weeks as signs of a slowdown in British growth have made analysts more confident in calling the top of the interest rate cycle. \par \par \uc2  British October producer price figures are due for release at 0930 GMT. Economists polled by Reuters expect input prices to have fallen a seasonally-adjusted 0.6 percent on the month, due to an easing in crude oil prices, but to have risen 12.9 percent on the year. \par \par \uc2  Other British data due this week include October retail price figures on Tuesday, labour market statistics on Wednesday and the Bank of England's quarterly inflation report on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Chartists at RedTower Research said sterling should see support at $1.4225 and resistance at $1.4365, and then $1.4400. Support on the euro/sterling cross is seen at 60.20 pence and resistance at 60.75 pence. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwbd0238o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
