\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro soft, awaits cliffhanger U.S. election recount. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Jucca \par \uc2 716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2000\par 11:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 9 (Reuters) - The euro was soft near one-week lows against the dollar on Thursday as dealers waited for the result of a recount in the key state of Florida which will decide who will be the next president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But lingering fears of renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were preventing the euro from sliding too far, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The recount is expected to be completed at around 2200 GMT but the dollar's firmness suggested traders were betting on a victory by Republican George W. Bush. \par \par \uc2  If Bush wins the White House, the euro is seen losing ground as a Republican administration was expected to promote a looser, more business-friendly, fiscal policy and be more reluctant at the idea of euro-supportive joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by the Group of Seven (G7) industrial countries. \par \par \uc2  "At the end of the day, we are still looking for Bush to take it, so we could see a bit more downward pressure on euro/dollar," said Mike Moran, treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to one-week lows around $0.8515 on Wednesday, shedding half a cent within a few minutes, after U.S. television networks projected Bush had won the election. \par \par \uc2  But the euro later trimmed its losses after Democratic candidate Al Gore, who had initially conceded victory to his opponent, withdrew his concession on grounds that the Florida's vote was too close to call. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said market action, which so far has been fairly subdued, could resume aggressively after the recount is completed. \par \par \uc2  But if absentee ballots mailed in from overseas were needed to determine the outcome, it could take until those ballots are counted on November 17 for the results to be finalised. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said the euro could get a lift if Florida overturned earlier projections and Democratic candidate Al Gore won the presidency. \par \par \uc2  "But such an outcome seems unlikely, especially since there are said to be more Republican supporters among the military personnel who will be sending in many of the absentee ballots," said a trust bank trader in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  At 0840 GMT, the euro was hovering within one third of a cent of one-week lows it had set on Wednesday and three cents above last month's record lows around $0.8225. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was holding a third of a yen above Wednesday's one-week lows around 91.40 yen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  STILL A POSSIBILITY \par \par \uc2  Although generally soft, the euro was getting some reprieve from wariness of more euro-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the ECB. \par \par \uc2  The G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop the euro on September 22 and the ECB conducted unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the euro last Friday and on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "Considering what the ECB might do, particularly in European markets, it's difficult to sell euros too much," said another dealer at a Japanese bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said recent comments by European policy-makers were confusing the market over the ultimate objective of the ECB's market action. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, ECB Chief Economist Otmar Issing said the latest ECB's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were a success and was upbeat on the euro zone's economic outlook despite recent soft data. \par \par \uc2  "This is not a helpful comment. It suggests the ECB's objectives were not great given that the euro is one big figure below the levels it was at before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  "Maybe their objective is to reduce the weakness of the euro rather than to instigate the recovery." \par \par \uc2  On the other hand Bundesbank's President Ernst Welteke was quoted on Wednesday as saying the euro was 25 to 30 percent undevalued against the dollar and that he was concerned about its persistent weakness. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile dollar/yen, largely sidelined, continued to trade within the boundaries of this week's narrow range. It stood at around 107.25 yen at 0840 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere sterling was soft across the board ahead of the end of a monetary policy meeting by the Bank of England as Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown's pre-budget statement, although generous, did little to change expectations of steady rates at the meeting. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwb902czz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
