\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-US election may be a factor in no euro aid. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Glenn Somerville \par \uc2 896 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2000\par 19:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Nov 3 (Reuters) - U.S. presidential elections just four days away may have played a role in keeping the United States from jumping aboard the latest rescue effort to prop up the ailing euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some analysts said Friday. \par \par \uc2  But if so it was just one of several factors that kept the U.S. Treasury and Federal Reserve on the sidelines as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  went into markets - on its own this time - buying euros to initially drive it up 1.5 cents from recent all-time lows to near 88 cents. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the United States didn't have a good reason to join in this time, as it did six weeks ago on Sept. 22 when a much broader coordinated euro bailout was launched by major economies around the globe. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly we will do it for our friends in Europe if there is a crisis but in general, a U.S. administration is going to be very cautious in these matters," said economist Pierre Ellis of Decision Economics Inc. NO CRISIS TO FORCE U.S. HAND \par \par \uc2  "In this case there was not a crisis, and the fact of the election so near was icing on the cake," Ellis suggested. \par \par \uc2  A U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the euro, which essentially would mean weakening the dollar's value against it, could potentially raise questions in the final days of intense campaigning about why a U.S. administration would want to drive down its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers issued a brief statement hours after the ECB's move, saying the United States stood by "our long standing dollar policy" but said he also shared Europe's concern "about the implications of the broad movements in the euro for the world economy." \par \par \uc2  The euro shed some of its early gains after the ECB said that neither the U.S. Federal Reserve nor other Group of Seven central banks outside the euro zone were involved in Friday's buying. The Fed would act at the U.S. Treasury's request in any such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as it did on Sept. 22. \par \par \uc2  Summers had said after the September \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped because there was a glaring market imbalance that was driving the euro down. \par \par \uc2  But there was no sign of market panic in recent days in the euro's slow slide in value, leaving the United States room to stay on the sidelines, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Economist Mark Vitner of First Union Corp. in Charlotte, N.C., said it was improbable there was any U.S. policy against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a pre-election period but he said the European action was likely as well-timed as possible considering it was acting on its own. \par \par \uc2  "There's a good chance that any time there's a change in U.S. administrations, with less certainty about future economic policy, that there'll be some vulnerability," Vitner said, so that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets might be more willing to hold the euro or other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they size up U.S. prospects. \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  VALUE QUESTIONED \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  never works but at least it can have a longer-term effect when it's well-timed as this one was," he added. \par \par \uc2  Some economists said the impending U.S. vote was not a consideration in staying out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , noting that Japan also stayed out and there were sound reasons to doubt it would work anyway. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think there was an election-related spin to the U.S. staying out," said economist Carl Weinberg of High Frequency Economics in New York, describing it as a European initiative that was unlikely to brace the euro for long. \par \par \uc2  "I think the U.S. was an unhappy participant in the last one (in September) and with no G7 confab on the horizon to force its hand the United States likely would take the same decision a week from today as it has today," he added. \par \par \uc2  The United States has followed a "strong-dollar policy" throughout the Clinton administration, which Summers quickly repeated after the September \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as he did again on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, Summers allowed in an interview that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can play a useful role in markets, but the U.S. position has essentially been to pressure Europe to do more to open and de-regulate its economies - making them more like the United States. \par \par \uc2  Ellis noted that there were good reasons for the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be cautious as a general matter in joining \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at propping up the euro's value when U.S. trade imbalances with the rest of the world are huge. \par \par \uc2  "You can't go trashing your own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when you're depending upon the rest of the world to send you funds to maintain your current account," Ellis said. \par \par \uc2  Economist Lynn Reaser of Banc of America Capital Management Inc. in St. Louis, Mo., said September's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  hadn't succeeded in keeping the euro buoyant and she doubted this one would either. \par \par \uc2  She said the U.S. strong-dollar policy was based on solid needs - for strong inflow of capital from abroad to finance a huge flow of imported goods, keeping a damper on import prices and so helping to hold U.S. interest rates down. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwb302let\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
