\par\par \b \uc2  IMM  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  end mostly higher on GDP-based gains. \b0\par\par\uc2 602 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2000\par 22:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, Oct 27 (Reuters) - New signs of a slowdown in U.S. economic growth and diminished yet still-present fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  drove December euros and most other IMM \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures into a strong close on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The December euro contract bounced as much as $0.015300 on Friday to one-week highs and closed up more than 1 cent after hitting a new record low and then firming on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Bearish sentiment toward December euro futures has started to weaken, and several analysts predicted the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will rebound in the week ahead as investors cover short positions before the November 7 U.S. presidential election. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Commerce Department said the nation's gross domestic product rose by 2.7 percent in the third quarter, less than half the 5.6 percentage gain posted in the prior quarter and well below forecasts for a 3.4 percent increase. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the surprisingly soft GDP data reinforced current market perceptions that the U.S. Federal Reserve may be finished raising interest rates and may in fact ease monetary policy by the beginning of next year. \par \par \uc2  "The thinking on the euro seems to have switched a little bit here on the GDP number," one floor broker said. "We'll see if that pans out for next week - $0.85000 would be a level that they're looking at on the December contract for a possible target." \par \par \uc2  Market participants conceded that the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  euro-positive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in collaboration with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely increase after the election. \par \par \uc2  December yen futures were battered lower by significant losses in Japan's benchmark Nikkei stock average and by data suggesting the nation's industrial production reversed its forward momentum in September. Although analysts said the data does not signal a change in the nation's trend of steady economic recovery, yen futures proved unable to take advantage of the soft U.S. economic data. \par \par \uc2  The December yen contract hit its lowest level since Monday, dropping as far as $0.009266, down $0.000044, ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "The yen still seems to be in a world of its own," one broker said. "I would think that as the euro goes up, it's going to get hurt on the (euro/yen) cross," he added. \par \par \uc2  Next week's economic data calendar is jam-packed. The Bank of Japan will hold a monetary policy meeting on Monday, while Japanese September employment, housing starts and construction data are scheduled for release on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The ECB will also announce its decision on interest rates next Thursday. Fifty-five out of 58 economists polled by Reuters predicted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would leave its rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  U.S. economic reports are due out every day next week and will be highlighted by Wednesday's National Association of Purchasing Management index and U.S. Federal Beige Book summary, as well as October payroll figures slated for Friday. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters predicted the October NAPM figure would once again come in below the 50.0 level, as it has for the past two months. A reading below 50.0 suggests economic contraction, while a reading above 50.0 suggests expansion. \par \par \uc2  December yen settled off $0.000022 at $0.009288, euros rallied $0.01080 to $0.84170, Swiss francs rose $0.0042 to $0.5561, sterling rose $0.0194 to $1.4550, Australian dollars firmed $0.0031 to $0.5241, Mexican pesos rallied $0.000775 to $0.102275 and Canadian dollars were $0.0017 lower at $0.6552. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwar03bos\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
