\par\par \b \uc2  POLL-US funds sit tight at home as euro crumples. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Priest \par \uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2000\par 12:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Oct 27 (Reuters) - U.S.-based fund managers are keeping their global bets steady, reluctant to shift out of shaky U.S. stock markets over the last month while the euro is still sliding. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of 11 U.S. asset allocators, conducted Monday through Thursday and released on Friday, found fund managers shaved their average allocations to U.S. equities to 44 percent of global stock portfolios from 45 percent in September's survey and doubled cash positions to 4 percent from 2 percent. \par \par \uc2  Despite the convulsions in U.S. stock markets which sent technology shares skidding lower on worries about the pace of profit growth, further crumpling of the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made it unappealing for U.S. funds to shift cash across the Atlantic. \par \par \uc2  Allocations to European shares remain little changed and fund managers kept their holdings of Japanese shares unchanged after three of months of cutting back bets there. \par \par \uc2  Over the last month, the U.S. Nasdaq composite has tumbled 19.6 percent, the Dow Jones industrial average has fallen 7.8 percent, the Japanese Nikkei 225 index has slid 13.5 percent and the Dow Jones STOXX index has fallen 6.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  But the euro has fallen 7.8 percent over the last two months against the dollar. This brings its total losses for the year to 19.2 percent. For U.S. funds this would have easily wiped out any positive returns in European markets this year. \par \par \uc2  A Group of 20 finance ministers communique made no mention of the euro's plight on Wednesday and a solitary comment from Mexican Finance Minister Jose Angel Gurria that the G20 agreed the euro was misaligned did little to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. \par \par \uc2  The Group of Seven top industrial nations spent up to an estimated $6 billion Sept. 22 to buy euros in a bid to halt its precipitous decline, but the euro soon resumed its stately downward progression. \par \par \uc2  Market speculation about U.S. participation in fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been rife since European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg, in a newspaper interview last week, appeared to play down the prospects of near-term \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and seemed to encourage the view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not join in any more euro-supportive action. \par \par \uc2  Charles Van Vleet, senior fund manager at Credit Suisse First Boston, said one of the main reasons for the euro's continued fall was the size of the U.S. capital account deficit which reflected huge foreign direct investment by European companies in the United States. \par \par \uc2  "To see the euro recover you are going to have to see Europe stop buying so much in the U.S., not the U.S. buying in Europe," he said. \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't seem that the market is very bunched up on the short side against the euro. It's pretty unlikely that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would catch many offside if they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  which makes it less likely to be successful," he said. \par \par \uc2  Most fund managers are underweight U.S. stocks vs. the Morgan Stanley Capital International World Index, a well followed benchmark stock index which gives recommended allocations to global bourses based on relative market capitalisation. \par \par \uc2  Economic data stretching back to late May has raised hopes among investors that the Fed's interest rate tightening cycle which began in June 1999 has succeeded in slowing the giant $10 trillion U.S. economy to a sustainable pace and removing the need for additional rate hikes. Rates currently stand at 6.50 percent \par \par \uc2  U.S. government data released on Thursday showed the pay and benefits of workers rose modestly in the third quarter of the year, in a report likely to further comfort the inflation-wary Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwar03b0i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
