\par\par \b \uc2  European silence weighs heavily on euro. \b0\par\par\uc2 339 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2000\par 23:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Oct 25 (Reuters) - As the euro hit fresh record lows against the dollar on Wednesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders waited in vain for at least a word or two of support from European finance ministers huddled at a conference in Montreal. \par \par \uc2  The Group of 20 (G20) industrialised and developing nations studiously avoided mentioning the euro in their closing seven-page communique, frustrating traders desperate for a clue about when central banks might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to help the euro. \par \par \uc2  "No action taken (by the G20), nor any strong words in defence of the euro has prompted a new sell-off," said John Schein, vice president at Fortis USA. \par \par \uc2  Germany's Finance Minister Hans Eichel and Bank of France Governor Jean-Claude Trichet both refused to discuss the euro. \par \par \uc2  Particularly surprising to New York traders was the studied silence of European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) President Wim Duisenberg, whose comments on the euro just last week were used as an excuse the pummel the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Finally, after U.S. markets closed for the day, Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke repeated the standard line that the euro's exchange rate does not reflect Europe's economic fundamentals. Welteke added that countries watching the euro's rates would respond appropriately. \par \par \uc2  MARKETS ASSESS CHANCES OF \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Amid the silence, dealers focused on what actually has been said. On Tuesday, U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers reaffirmed the long-standing policy that a strong dollar was in U.S. interest. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday's comments by Larry Summers...indicate that you still don't have any consensus among the G7 central bankers," said Alex Beuzelin, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market analyst at Ruesch International. \par \par \uc2  Last week Duisenberg appeared in a newspaper interview to downplay prospects of near-term \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , seeming to encourage the view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not join further euro-supportive action. \par \par \uc2  And with the U.S. Presidential elections only two weeks away, analysts said it was increasingly unlikely the U.S. Federal Reserve will consider acting until the vote is in. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwap03g27\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
