\par\par \b \uc2  Euro pinned near life low as market awaits G20. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 1,019 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2000\par 08:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 24 (Reuters) - The euro was pinned near lifetime lows on Tuesday as the market all but dared the world's major powers to come rescue the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  With a Group of 20 meeting just about to start in Montreal, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still hovering a quarter cent above its lifetime low as bears bet that such a large gathering would produce little in the way of concrete support. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that Deputy German Finance Minister Caio Koch-Weser had confirmed as much on Monday when he said that there would be no statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather something on "vulnerabilities" in the world economy. \par \par \uc2  "The market's looking for at least an official statement of support for the euro from the G7 and if they don't get one, it'll be another reason to sell," Lindsay Gordon, a G7 analyst at IDEAglobal.com in Singapore, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  But even if such a statement was forthcoming, analysts doubted it would deter euro bears for long. \par \par \uc2  "It's very hard to turn the euro around right now. There's simply an absence of faith in it and the ECB (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," added Gordon. \par \par \uc2  At 0545 GMT, the euro was at $0.8363/66 having fallen from above 84 cents on Monday and leaving it uncomfortably close to last week's $0.8324 record low. \par \par \uc2  The euro's woes spread to the Swiss franc, where the dollar leapt to an 11-year high on Monday around 1.8000 francs. It faded to 1.7948/58 by late Tokyo trade, leading to speculation that the Swiss National Bank might be offering dollars above 1.8000. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, sterling was developing a niche as a European safe-haven, helped in part by its status as a petro-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It advanced to a five-month high on the euro near 0.5748/54 pence. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was pegged at 108.20 yen in late trade from around 109.00 early on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Traders reported talk of a very large buy order in the 108.15/20 area, but also of sizeable stop-loss offers under 108.10, which would tend to attract speculative sellers. \par \par \uc2  There was some anticipation of offshore demand for yen related to the Japanese government's NTT share offer. Foreign investors are being offered about $3.0 billion of shares and must pay by November 9. \par \par \uc2  However, traders said it was possible foreign investors would opt to keep their overall Japanese share weightings steady by selling other Japanese equities to buy NTT. \par \par \uc2  Traders reported increasing whispers of massive \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses at some Japanese institutions, particularly insurers, related to their euro exposure. \par \par \uc2  "It's been common knowledge for a long time that they were under water on their euro debt, but now some figures are leaking out and they're frightening," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  He said rumours were that one insurer alone had lost the equivalent of $1.0 billion, another around $800 million while a third had "only" lost $300 million thanks to aggressive hedging. \par \par \uc2  Such huge \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses were a major reason Japanese investors remained extremely reluctant to invest abroad even though returns there were far higher than they could earn at home. \par \par \uc2  "Without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro may come under fresh downward pressure. At the moment, many operators have their positions squared off," a Japanese city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "We have to listen to officials from Europe and the United States at the meeting on whether their concerns are strong about the weakening euro. If their concerns are not strong enough, speculators will start selling again." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market could probe a lifetime low $0.8324 and push the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  towards $0.80. \par \par \uc2  But the market's focus may shift to a series of important U.S. economic figures, including Thursday's employment cost report and Friday's third quarter growth data, they said. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. share markets are still very vulnerable. There is a danger that the economic data could shake the market and hurt the dollar," the dealer said. "In this situation, it's important to see the stance of the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the G20 meeting." \par \par \uc2  CLEAR TREND AFTER G20 \par \par \uc2  "Without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro may come under fresh downward pressure. At the moment, many operators have their positions squared off," a Japanese city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "We have to listen to officials from Europe and the United States at the meeting on whether their concerns are strong about the weakening euro. If their concerns are not strong enough, speculators will start selling again." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market could probe a lifetime low $0.8324 and push the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  towards $0.80. \par \par \uc2  But the market's focus may shift to a series of important U.S. economic figures, including Thursday's employment cost report and Friday's third quarter growth data, they said. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. share markets are still very vulnerable. There is a danger that the economic data could shake the market and hurt the dollar," the dealer said. "In this situation, it's important to see the stance of the United States at the G20 meeting." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0624 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   23 Oct.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab   0.8357   0.8352   +0.06   -17.01   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab    108.20   108.22   -0.02    +5.86   102.21\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab    90.40    90.44   -0.04   -12.32   103.10\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab  1.7970   1.7954   +0.09   +13.00   1.5902\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab    1.4538   1.4539   -0.01   -10.13   1.6176\par  Can/dlr\tab \tab    1.5096   1.5106   -0.07    +4.43   1.4455\par  Aus/dlr\tab \tab    0.5255   0.5264   -0.17   -19.93   0.6563\par  Euro/swiss\tab \tab 1.5015   1.5027   -0.08    -6.37   1.6036\par  Euro/stg\tab \tab   0.5743   0.5743   +0.00    -7.95   0.6239.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwao03l2p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
