\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Oil prices seen becoming issue at G20 talks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2000\par 20:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MONTREAL, Oct 24 (Reuters) - The need to strengthen fragile markets will dominate the formal part of top-level financial talks in Canada this week, but the fight for stable oil prices will nudge its way into the talks, delegates said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  A senior Canadian finance official, briefing reporters before the start of the annual meeting of the rich-country, emerging-market Group of 20, said oil prices were not a formal topic at the two-day gathering, which starts on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But he added: "My expectation is that people will want to have a discussion on how to stabilise prices." \par \par \uc2  Oil producers Mexico and Saudi Arabia are two members of the G20, which was set up last year to give emerging markets a louder voice in international financial affairs of state. \par \par \uc2  They are reaping the benefits of a steep run-up in the cost of oil, while other emerging markets are suffering from higher import bills and worsening terms of trade. Oil traded at around $33.75 a barrel on Tuesday, about $4 below last month's 10-year high, but way above the $20 to $25 a barrel price range sought by the Clinton administration. \par \par \uc2  The meeting, already dogged by protests like the ones that disrupted trade talks in Seattle last year, also takes place against the backdrop of volatile stock markets and a fragile European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro. \par \par \uc2  But participants on Tuesday played down market expectations that they might consider new proposals on things like support for the euro and some delegates said it was not even clear if a press statement would be released after two days of talks. \par \par \uc2  LET'S JUST TALK \par \par \uc2  "This is a forum for an exchange of ideas," Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke told Reuters. "This isn't really a forum for any concrete policy initiatives." \par \par \uc2  Wim Duisenberg, the head of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , declined to comment on the battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when he arrived in Montreal, and the Canadian finance official said any comment on any exchange rate would be inappropriate. \par \par \uc2  "I'm not going to make any comment about individual \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  or exchange rates. I think for me to do so would not be helpful for the G20, the international financial system or my own career prospects," he said. \par \par \uc2  Italian Treasury Secretary Vincenzo Visco insisted that the main problem was to persuade financial markets that the European economy was strong. Asked what Europe might do to lift the euro, he said, "You are asking whether it has moral and political support, yes this kind of support is there." \par \par \uc2  The euro has been bouncing around near record lows against the U.S. dollar, reaping no long-term support from a surprise round of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  joined that action. But U.S. officials have long doubted whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  works. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers on Tuesday repeated his tried and tested line that a strong U.S. dollar was in the best interests of the United States. \par \par \uc2  The G20 meetings are to take place behind closed doors in a well-guarded downtown Montreal hotel. Front and side doors were locked on Monday, at the height of anti-globalization protests, but delegates were still able to enter and leave the building through a side entrance. \par \par \uc2  Further demonstrations have been called for Tuesday by the Council of Canadians, an independent, nonpartisan interest group that focuses on social and economic issues and on asserting Canadian sovereignty. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwao03k4u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
