\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Summers backs strong dollar, shares G7 euro concern. \b0\par\par\uc2 373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2000\par 03:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Oct 18 (Reuters) - Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers on Wednesday said the strong dollar is in the national interest, but repeated that the United States shared Europe's concern over its struggling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Speaking just hours after the euro plunged to record lows against the dollar, Summers said that while the "strong dollar" policy remained in force, the U.S. position is also reflected in a statement issued by the Group of Seven wealthy nations on the risks a weak euro poses to the global economy. \par \par \uc2  "In our judgment the fundamentals of the U.S. economy are sound. The G7 in its communique spoke to our position on exchange rates, and of course our policy of the strong dollar is in the national interest is unchanged," Summers told reporters after addressing a business conference. \par \par \uc2  Summers declined to comment on U.S. readiness to support a fresh round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro. Four weeks ago the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  joined other G7 countries in a high-profile \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has given up all of its gains since then. \par \par \uc2  The G7 communique, issued shortly after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , spoke of "the shared concern...about the potential implications of recent movements in the euro for the world economy," adding that members would continue to monitor developments and cooperate again if needed. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Summers appeared to leave the door open to the U.S. to joining in another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while being careful not to destabilise the dollar in a time of stock market volatility. \par \par \uc2  "He doesn't want to rule out any U.S. participation in any new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ashraf Laidi, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at MG Financial Group in New York. \par \par \uc2  "But by the same token, if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  happens again, this reiteration of the strong dollar policy will help avoid misinterpretations or confusion which would potentially lead to a decline in the dollar," said Laidi. \par \par \uc2  His comments came as the dollar vaulted to fresh record highs against the stumbling euro, which tumbled 2.5 percent in the U.S. trading session to a low at 83.28 cents. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwaj040ti\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
