\par\par \b \uc2  Markets puzzle over G7 message as euro flounders. \b0\par\par\uc2  By James Saft \par \uc2 673 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2000\par 12:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 19 (Reuters) - A rash of contradictory and unusual statements by Group of Seven policy makers left market players scratching their heads on Thursday over the future fortunes of both the ailing single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and international coordination. \par \par \uc2  The euro struggled above fresh record lows around $0.8325 set overnight after a second U.S. central banker spoke out about the costs for U.S. industry of the strong dollar, a policy Treasury Secretary Summers later took pains to endorse. \par \par \uc2  All this comes as markets are braced for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision on interest rates at 1145 GMT and what is seen as a key press conference afterwards for its President Wim Duisenberg, who has been embroiled in controversy over his remarks which seemed to limit the chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There is a lot of noise, a lot of lack of coordination but for that you don't need to look outside the euro area, but within the euro area as well," said Alfonso Prat-Gay, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at J.P. Morgan in London. \par \par \uc2  "We never thought \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was going to work for many reasons, one of them was lack of coordination." \par \par \uc2  San Francisco Federal Reserve President Robert Parry said on Wednesday the a strong dollar was pinching U.S. companies and curbing overseas demand for U.S. products, echoing comments made by colleague Alfred Broaddus the day before. \par \par \uc2  Summers on Wednesday said the strong dollar was in the national interest, but repeated that the United States shared Europe's concern over the risks a weak euro poses to the global economy. \par \par \uc2  Summers declined to comment on U.S. readiness to support a fresh round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro. Four weeks ago the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  joined other G7 countries in a high-profile \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has given up all of its gains since then. \par \par \uc2  MIYAZAWA TIPS HAND \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Thursday he has not heard anything from euro-zone authorities about the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the ailing euro. \par \par \uc2  Asked if he thought a coordinated effort was needed to help the euro, which fell to fresh record lows overnight, Miyazawa said: "That is up to the euro authorities. I have not heard anything about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from them." \par \par \uc2  But he added that Japanese authorities would carefully watch the euro's movements. \par \par \uc2  Central bankers have traditionally avoided discussing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which rely in large measure on the element of surprise for success. \par \par \uc2  "It is puzzling that Miyazawa would say that," said Steven Englander, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Schroder Salomon Smith Barney in London. \par \par \uc2  "But you don't know, it could be a smoke screen." \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa's remarks, in their frankness, brought back memories of Duisenberg's interview Monday in London's Times newspaper in which the ECB head was reported to have minimised the prospect of more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the euro, sparking another slide for the hapless \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts have not taken issue so much with his comments, that the G7 would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if a severe Middle East crisis sparked a fall in the euro's value, as with the wisdom of commenting publicly on such a policy. \par \par \uc2  "His only crime was to state the blindingly obvious - that the U.S. was less likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a background of falling asset markets ahead of a Presidential election," said Nick Parsons, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Commerzbank in London. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said they will be particularly sensitive to any comments from Duisenberg at the post rate decision press conference, especially following speculation that he would step down prematurely. \par \par \uc2  Markets will also be potentially even more volatile as the busy ECB agenda comes as Fed Chairman Greenspan is scheduled to speak at 1310. As well Germany's Ifo research institute said on Thursday that the release of its September business climate index would be delayed until at least 1200 GMT, if not Friday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwaj03rxz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
