\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro seems fated to fall further as ECB flounders. \b0\par\par\uc2 838 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2000\par 04:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 17 (Reuters) - The euro seemed fated to fall further on Tuesday, having survived a brush with record lows only by the skin of its teeth. \par \par \uc2  The much-maligned \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hovering at $0.8497 in late morning trade after sinking to $0.8459 in New York, its fourth straight day of post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lows and only just above the lifetime trough of $0.8440. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Monday's bearishly construed comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg still reverberated through the market, drawing criticism from analysts and the media and damaging the bank's already tattered credibility. \par \par \uc2  Dealers even interpreted the silence of U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers as proof of a split between him and Duisenberg on the strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  In his all-too-frank interview with the Times, Duisenberg revealed that he had asked Summers to modify his language when referring to the long-standing strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers assumed the request was ignored since Summers made a point of re-stating the policy the same day as the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Asked about this late on Monday Summers declined all comment, perhaps aware of the potential pitfalls of being frank with the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but that merely fed the market's suspicion that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not 100 percent behind \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  DAMAGE LIMITATION \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi tried to paper over the cracks on Tuesday, telling reporters that the Group of Seven will cooperate in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as stated at last month's G7 ministers' meeting. \par \par \uc2  He added a further fall in the euro would be inappropriate and would not reflect Europe's economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Furthermore, the Japanese financial newspaper Nihon Keizai Shimbun in a brief report datelined Frankfurt quoted an ECB source saying the ECB would take a flexible approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it slid further on Mideast tensions. This stood in contrast to Duisenberg's comment that G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in reaction to an external event like a Mideast war would be unlikely. \par \par \uc2  "All smacks of a damage limitation exercise," said a European bank dealer. "But it can't be good for credibility when the ECB has to scramble to clear up after its boss all the time." \par \par \uc2  Paul Goldi, deputy manager of treasury at Commerzbank, argued that the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still real given the credibility of the whole G7 was at risk and not just the ECB. \par \par \uc2  But Duisenberg's comments had made the job all the harder. \par \par \uc2  "Next time, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to be very successful; drive the euro up above 88 or even 90 and keep it there, or the market will just keep testing them," said Goldi. \par \par \uc2  "As a result, I think the market firmly believes the euro will only recover on its own and not through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  HOW LOW CAN IT GO? \par \par \uc2  Which is why he expected a test of the record lows around $0.8440, a break of which could trigger stop-loss sell orders at 84 cents and take it to 82 if not 80 cents. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also subdued on the yen at 91.74 yen although it did survive an early test of key support at 91.60. In contrast the dollar was holding steady at 107.97 yen , having ended at 108.02 in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro was not the only \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  being steamrollered by the dollar. Both the Australian and New Zealand dollars hit record troughs early on Tuesday while the Canadian dollar sank to its lowest level since March of last year. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc was also in retreat as investors were all too quick to unwind safe haven positions established last week when U.S. equities were under pressure. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stretched as high as 1.7835 francs in New York on Monday, a rise of five centimes in just two days and within spitting distance of decade peaks around 1.7920. \par \par \uc2  It subsequently eased back to 1.7735 francs with dealers reporting rumours the Swiss National Bank had sold dollars as part of its ongoing liquidation of gold reserves. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0235 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   16 Oct.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab   0.8496   0.8498   -0.02   -15.63   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab    108.01   108.02   -0.01    +5.67   102.21\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab    91.77    91.86   -0.10   -10.99   103.10\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab  1.7733   1.7744   -0.06   +11.51   1.5902\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab    1.4457   1.4473   -0.11   -10.63   1.6176\par  Can/dlr\tab \tab    1.5178   1.5178   +0.00    +5.00   1.4455\par  Aus/dlr\tab \tab    0.5220   0.5224   -0.08   -20.46   0.6563\par  Eur