\par\par \b \uc2  Euro cure mainly Europe's problem, analysts say. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Glenn Somerville \par \uc2 716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2000\par 01:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Oct 5 (Reuters) - Any further spending of treasure by governments to support the wobbling euro will have to be initiated by Europe, but the United States would likely join in reluctantly again, analysts said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Europe's fledgling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under renewed pressure on Thursday despite a surprise move by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates a quarter percentage point. \par \par \uc2  European authorities denied the decision was linked to a Sept. 22 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Group of Seven industrial powers. However, they said they were ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buy euros again if necessary. \par \par \uc2  The rate-hike action initially brought the euro up about one third of a cent to nearly 88 cents. But it quickly softened as it dropped under 87 cents for the first time since Sept. 22, raising red flags about the possibility of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . TRADERS EYE EURO FOR WEAKNESS \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that just as in the first round, Europe would have to kick off any such concerted move but that it would likely need U.S. participation to have a daunting impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders who see the euro as vulnerable. \par \par \uc2  "Most people are very sceptical of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now but they would be especially so if it was done without the participation of the United States," said economist Paul Kasriel of Northern Trust Co. in Chicago. \par \par \uc2  Two weeks ago, the U.S. Treasury emphasised it was acting "at the initiative of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 " when it confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  had joined Japan, Europe and others in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying the euro to push up its value. \par \par \uc2  Summers later said the weak euro had posed a danger to the world economy and cited growing jitters in world \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that had prompted him to agree to help Europe. But he also insisted the move did not conflict with his longstanding preference for a "strong dollar", which he has credited for keeping inflation and interest rates down in the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve, as America's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , handles the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Treasury's direction. Coordinated actions like the one to help the euro are relatively rare, with the last one having come in 1998 to bolster the then-weak Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said the ECB's action in raising rates on Thursday was counter-productive for the euro. \par \par \uc2  Adam Posen, an economist with the Washington-based Institute for International Economics, said the basic issue was that the U.S. economy was simply growing much more vibrantly than those in Europe. \par \par \uc2  That means investors want to buy dollars to participate in the U.S. expansion and do not want to hold euros. \par \par \uc2  "There's no short-term way out of it," Posen said. "There's nothing credible the Fed and ECB can do." \par \par \uc2  Richard Yamarone, an economist with Argus Corp. in New York, similarly said that pushing European credit costs higher seemed unlikely to bolster European economic performance. \par \par \uc2  "If anything, the fundamentals are likely to be worsened by what the ECB has done today," Yamarone commented. EUROPE TOLD TO REFORM ITSELF \par \par \uc2  The euro was introduced in January 1999 and it debuted on the global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stage with a value of $1.17. But it has been pummeled lower, amid calls from the Clinton administration for Europe to reform its economies to make them a stronger-performing partner so that the U.S. economy need not be a sole growth engine for the rest of the world. \par \par \uc2  Posen said Europe was taking some of the needed corrective actions, like cutting taxes in Europe, that should help the region's growth and narrow the gap with the United States. \par \par \uc2  That would eventually help the euro, but it will take time for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to find its value. \par \par \uc2  "Trying to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now is going to be a waste," Posen said. "They haven't got enough money, and the ECB trying to gut growth (by raising rates) just isn't going to credible. \par \par \uc2  "They're going to have to bite the bullet (on reforms)." \par \par \uc2  U.S. authorities, including Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan, have also expressed reservations about the long-term utility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , viewing them as likely to have only temporary impact unless real reforms are made. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwa5055w8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
