\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Euro heads for critical quarter with G7 backing. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 909 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2000\par 15:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 29 (Reuters) - Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  posed enough of a threat to the world economy in the last three months to force the Group of Seven nations into action, but the rest of the year will show whether its dog days are finally over. \par \par \uc2  The G7 clubbed together to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign exchanges for the first time in five years after the euro's fall gathered fresh momentum in September, and subsequently warned that they would keep working together whenever they saw fit. \par \par \uc2  Their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  shocked dealers who had not expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to throw its weight behind such action and halted a decline that had seen the euro hit record lows against the dollar on eight separate trading days this month. \par \par \uc2  Respect for the wishes of the world's richest nations even outweighed traders' inclinations to batter the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of Denmark's rejection of euro entry - an outcome which had been expected to deal it a severe psychological blow. \par \par \uc2  The key question now is whether the G7 can keep the euro afloat long enough into the fourth quarter for it to sprout its own wings. \par \par \uc2  "Watersheds generally come after we have had a long slide in a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and we get joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - when central banks show they are jointly committed to something, you generally start to see a turnaround," said Nigel Richardson, investment strategist at AXA Investment Managers. \par \par \uc2  "People are reluctant to say we have seen the bottom in the euro because we have had so many disappointments but the risk has got to be that it is going higher in the coming quarter." \par \par \uc2  PR DISASTERS A THING OF THE PAST? \par \par \uc2  G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was launched on the initiative of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has gone some way to undoing some of the damage done to the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by public relations gaffes committed by some of the most senior figures in the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder was one of the prime culprits as his publicly-expressed thesis that the euro's fall was more a cause for satisfaction than concern was responsible for tipping it to record lows in the third quarter. \par \par \uc2  More life lows were to follow until joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  finally halted the slide. While fear of more G7 action has so far kept the euro away from these troughs, one unguarded comment too many could embolden traders once again. \par \par \uc2  "You have things like Schroeder saying a weak euro was quite good so the credibility of euro zone policymakers is not great," said Glenn Davies, chief economist at Credit Lyonnais, London. \par \par \uc2  But if the euro's fall and the inflation headaches it was causing the ECB were hitting the headlines on one side of the Atlantic in the third quarter, profit warnings from U.S. firms were big news on the other. \par \par \uc2  U.S. firms' woes could give the euro just the shot in the arm it needs in the final three months of the year as the flip side of its protracted fall has been the dollar's rampant strenth. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been rallying for five years and trampled over more than just the sinking euro in the process. It hit its highest in over a decade against the Swiss franc and sterling in the third quarter and 15-year peaks on a trade-weighted basis. \par \par \uc2  Such protracted strength has led to a widening in the U.S. current account deficit but financial markets have turned a blind eye to the shortfall while they knew international investors would keep snapping up U.S. stocks, bonds, and firms. \par \par \uc2  Their faith in the dollar's ability to defy the gravitational pull of the U.S. current account deficit has so far proved justified. \par \par \uc2  "We came into this year thinking that the turning point in the euro could be a fall in U.S. stock markets but it hasn't happened yet, so people are not necessarily working on that assumption," said Graham Cocks, manager, corporate treasury at Mellon Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  But the toll that the dollar's strength is taking on U.S. competitiveness is becoming harder to ignore as it keeps cropping up in the profit warnings from corporate America that have begun to rattle Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  Such jitters require a steady hand at the tiller, and while Alan Greenspan is set to remain at the helm of the U.S. Federal Reserve, it is still unclear who will be in the White House after the U.S. Presidential elections in early November. \par \par \uc2  Investors' confidence in U.S. assets has been underpinned by the steadfastness with which the U.S. Treasury has stuck by its strong dollar policy and built up a budget surplus. \par \par \uc2  Any changes to government policy on either front would therefore rattle financial markets and the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The fourth quarter will be a testing time for the dollar as there will be a new administration in the U.S. and the market will be watching U.S. stocks and data carefully," said Neil Mackinnon, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  "The market will also be looking to see whether investor confidence in the euro is rebuilt in the aftermath of the G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but you take on the G7 at your peril. A floor has been established and we are advising clients to buy the euro." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9t05w48\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
