\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-G7 generosity surprises but more euro buying needed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2000\par 15:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 28 (Reuters) - The Group of Seven nations may have been more extravagant than believed when they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the euro's defence last week, but their commitment to the euro's cause will be judged on their willingness to spend more money. \par \par \uc2  Early indications of how deeply they dug into their pockets began to emerge by Thursday, with one report putting the total amount at about six billion euros ($5.3 billion) and U.S. Treasury data prompting analysts to suggest the United States may have stumped up more than $1 billion. \par \par \uc2  Both figures were at the top end of the range traders had been bandying around and showed that the G7 meant to issue a stern protest at the euro's relentless fall, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  But they added that the G7 nations would have to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly to convince financial markets that they were determined to turn the tide in the euro's favour. \par \par \uc2  "These numbers are somewhat higher than the market thought and signals that they were serious," said Paul Meggyesi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "But their commitment is a function of their willingness to continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  together, as and when it is necessary, rather than the amount of money they spent." \par \par \uc2  G7 SPREE BIGGER THAN BELIEVED \par \par \uc2  A firm indication of how much the G7 spent buying euros on Friday has been hard to come by, as the central banks involved dealt with a wide variety of financial institutions in different countries and sold various major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for euros. \par \par \uc2  Estimates that the G7 had collectively spent upwards of $3 billion floated around after Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders had speculated that the U.S. had forked out the equivalent of less than $1 billion, in line with the amount it spent when it had last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  - on dollar/yen in 1998. \par \par \uc2  Latest ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve data, released on Tuesday, covered the week to September 22 but gave no indication of the size of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's euro purchases on Friday were settled after the reporting period. \par \par \uc2  But more up to date U.S. Treasury statistics suggest total U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday amounted to more than $1 billion, according to analysts. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. data came after Germany's weekly Die Zeit newspaper said in an unsourced report on Wednesday that central banks had spent the equivalent of six billion euros on Friday over the course of three rounds of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The numbers may be miniscule compared with daily turnover in the foreign exchanges, which is about $1.5 trillion, but central banks always get a bigger bang for their buck, particularly when they show their hand as publicly as they did on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "They would want to maximise price impact so they would want to hit the market with at least a couple of billion (dollars), but it is only a signal," said Peter von Maydell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in London. \par \par \uc2  "A few billion can have the same impact as tens of billions and you don't want to waste taxpayers' money." \par \par \uc2  This view has been backed up by events - the euro had set record lows against the dollar for four consecutive trading days in the week preceding \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but has since pulled about four cents away from its nadir. \par \par \uc2  The impact of Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was all the greater because the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  rode to the euro's rescue alongside other members of the G7. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. participation confounded those who had expected it would resist being implicated in any action which might scare off the international investors whose purchases of U.S. stocks and bonds help finance the burgeoning U.S. current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  But while the foreign exchanges were stunned to see the U.S. buying euros for dollars on its own account, dealers are now wondering whether its appearance was a one-off show of solidarity that might not be repeated. \par \par \uc2  Past experience suggests the G7 are not loath to make repeated forays into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets when they want to make a point and current sentiment suggests they might have to make more joint appearances in the euro's support, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Even if the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is confirmed as being somewhat bigger than the market had thought, it is not large enough to turn around the trend," said Alfonso Prat-Gay, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at J.P. Morgan in London. \par \par \uc2  "If the G7's target was to calm down moves in the euro, they have succeeded, but if it is a question of turning it around, they will need more than six billion euros." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9s068f7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
