\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro cushioned as G7  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fears linger. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2000\par 09:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 26 (Reuters) - The euro was kept afloat on Tuesday with traders reluctant to challenge the Group of Seven's commitment to support the euro following their joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its defence last week. \par \par \uc2  Caution ahead of Thursday's Danish referendum on euro entry was also said to be another factor supporting the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it held about three cents away from the record lows it hit against the dollar last week. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that while a "no" vote by the Danes was likely to deal a fresh blow to confidence in the euro, latest opinion polls showed that Danish euro opponents' lead over supporters had been shrinking. \par \par \uc2  "The fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has limited the euro downside and it is pretty likely they will come in again if the euro goes down," said Tony Norfield, head of global treasury research at ABN Amro Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "It's been kind of known for a while that the Danes are sceptical about EMU and if they say no, it will be a mild negative for the euro and if they say yes, it will be a mild positive." \par \par \uc2  Traders were now looking to the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weekly money market tender, due later in the day. The latest Reuters poll suggestss the average rate at the refinancing auction will rise to 4.64 percent and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers are watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  with interest to see whether it will sterilise last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro rose as far as $0.8799 EUR=) by 0746 gmt, up more than 3.5 percent from last week's record lows around $0.8440. However, it is still down more than two cents from Friday's post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  highs. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was holding just above 94 yen EURJPY=R. \par \par \uc2  Senior G7 officials continued to keep the possibility of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the forefront. Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa earlier on Tuesday issued a warning to the market of the possibility of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the euro plunges again. \par \par \uc2  "Even if people wanted to, nobody can sell euro now particularly after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and ahead of the Danish vote," said a Japanese commercial bank trader in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "The euro could come under renewed selling pressure if Denmark rejects the euro and that will be the moment to judge the G7 nations and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determination to prevent it sliding." \par \par \uc2  Despite a strong G7 statement of their commitment to stabilising the euro following Friday's surprise joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some traders still doubt the commitment of the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the euro at the expense of its long-standing "strong dollar" policy. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR SOFTENS VS YEN \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar softened against a generally firmer yen in thin trading ahead of the release of the Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" business sentiment survey next Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell as far as 107.23 yen JPY=), down about a yen from Friday's one-month highs around 108.15 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar/yen rate is clearly out of the market's focus now until the tankan data next week. Presently, the rate is being moved by the supply-demand balance and the fluctuations in the euro/yen rate," a Japanese trust bank dealer in Tokyo said. \par \par \uc2  Some yen-supportive news also emerged on Monday from ratings agency Standard and Poor's Corp (S&P), which said it saw no reason to downgrade Japan's sovereign debt rating from its current top triple-A status. \par \par \uc2  This follows Moody's Investors Service decision recently to downgrade Japan's rating for domestic yen-denominated bonds issued by the Japanese government to Aa2 from Aa1, putting the rating two notches below S&P's. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9q06epm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
