\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro buoyed amid wariness of G7  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Deepthi Wickremasinghe \par \uc2 809 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2000\par 12:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 26 (Reuters) - The euro was underpinned on Tuesday amid jitters of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity following the Group of Seven's joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \uc2  The market's nervousness was highlighted when the euro staged a swift rebound, racking up gains of half a yen and more than a third of a cent within minutes, after falling towards levels at which G7 central banks were spotted on Friday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wariness was later highlighted after comments from the the head of the International Monetary Fund Horst Koehler briefly pushed the euro to test its day's highs. \par \par \uc2  Koehler said that balanced exchange rates were needed and Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at bringing the euro more in line with fundamentals. "At some stage the G7 will come in again. The market seems to be suggesting a move below $0.86 towards $0.85 would be the impetus for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action again," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mistubishi in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro was within sight of its day's high at $0.8800 EUR=) according to Reuters data, up more than 3.5 percent from last week's record lows around $0.8440. However, it was still down more than two cents from Friday's post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  highs. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was holding above 94 yen EURJPY=), up more than four yen from last weeks' record lows. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weekly money market tender was inconclusive on the amount of Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that might have been sterilised but the higher than expected rates threw the focus forwards to Thursday's ECB policy meeting. \par \par \uc2  The ECB alloted refis in variable tender at a 4.65 percent marginal rate and a 4.68 percent average rate. \par \par \uc2  "It is difficult to ascertain the amount sterilised (from the ECB refi auction). The interest rate move up was a bit higher than people had expected and the key will be Thursday when there is a reasonable chance that we will get something," said Halpenny at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  EYES ON DANISH REFERENDUM \par \par \uc2  Caution ahead of Thursday's Danish referendum on euro entry was also said to be supporting the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it held about three cents away from the record lows it hit against the dollar last week. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that while a "no" vote by the Danes was likely to deal a fresh blow to confidence in the euro, latest opinion polls showed that Danish euro opponents' lead over supporters had been shrinking. \par \par \uc2  "The fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has limited the euro downside and it is pretty likely they will come in again if the euro goes down," said Tony Norfield, head of global treasury research at ABN AMRO Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "It's been kind of known for a while that the Danes are sceptical about EMU and if they say no, it will be a mild negative for the euro and if they say yes, it will be a mild positive." \par \par \uc2  Senior G7 officials continued to keep the possibility of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the forefront. Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa earlier on Tuesday issued a warning to the market of the possibility of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the euro plunges again. \par \par \uc2  Despite a strong G7 statement of their commitment to stabilising the euro following Friday's surprise joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said the the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the most reluctant participant in Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  given the U.S. authorities commitment to its long-standing "strong dollar" policy. \par \par \uc2  "Because U.S. policy of a strong dollar has been maintained, and the possibility that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't sizeable on Friday, it would suggest that they are more just trying to stem the decline rather than turn the euro around and boost it higher, which could undermine growth in the euro zone," Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi's Halpenny said. \par \par \uc2  He said euro/dollar could therefore range trade between $0.86 and $0.90 for some time. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar softened against a generally firmer yen in thin trading ahead of the release of the Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" business sentiment survey next Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to around 107.20 yen JPY=), down about a yen from Friday's one-month highs around 108.15 yen. \par \par \uc2  Some yen-supportive news emerged on Monday from ratings agency Standard and Poor's Corp (S&P), which said it saw no reason to downgrade Japan's sovereign debt rating from its current top triple-A status. \par \par \uc2  This followed Moody's Investors Service decision recently to downgrade Japan's rating for domestic yen-denominated bonds issued by the Japanese government to Aa2 from Aa1, putting the rating two notches below S&P's. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9q06cym\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfass